  "title": "Is America Preparing to Invade Iran? Follow the Logistics, Not the Threats.",
  "deck": "An invasion needs more than threats and airstrikes. EP News examines troop increases, sealift, equipment, medical support and contracts—and finds no verified public evidence yet of an imminent large-scale ground invasion.",
  image: { src: "assets/images/epnews_iran_logistics_revised_1600x900.png", alt: "EP News conceptual infographic on military logistics; ship imagery is synthetic and does not document a deployment", credit: "Epic Perspective News AI-generated conceptual illustration; synthetic ship scene is not documentary photography and must receive editorial authenticity approval." },
  "body": [
  "The question is logistics, not rhetoric. An air campaign and a large ground invasion require different military footprints. Airstrikes can be supported by aircraft, ships and regional bases; taking and holding Iranian territory would also demand ground combat units, protected supply lines, sustained transport, engineering and medical support. This investigation asks what the public record can establish, rather than treating warnings or readiness exercises as an invasion order.",
  "The baseline matters. The strongest indicator would be a documented net increase in ground combat power above ordinary rotations and existing regional deployments. A unit arriving while another departs is not necessarily a buildup. Public announcements should be compared by unit type, destination, departure date and operational role; classified movements may remain invisible.",
  "Sealift is the hard constraint. Heavy armored vehicles, ammunition, spare parts, engineering equipment and fuel require substantial transport and onward distribution. Analysts should distinguish ships routinely assigned to the region from newly activated sealift capacity, prepositioning operations and verified cargo deliveries. A ship movement alone cannot identify its cargo or mission.",
  "Medical and mortuary preparations need context. Field hospitals, casualty evacuation plans and mortuary-affairs exercises can support contingency planning for many theaters. The U.S. military trains for large-scale casualties even without a specific invasion plan. A persuasive Iran-specific signal would require verified forward deployments, unusual procurement or multiple corroborating indicators tied to the same theater.",
  "Follow contracts, but do not overread them. Federal contract notices can reveal demand for construction, logistics, sustainment, medical services or equipment. Yet routine maintenance and broad contingency contracts are not proof of an Iran operation. Track award dates, place of performance, quantities, delivery deadlines and whether the work adds capacity beyond established baselines.",
  "What recent strikes do—and do not—show. Reported strikes on Iranian territory, including around strategically important Gulf approaches, can indicate military pressure or preparation for additional options. They do not by themselves establish an amphibious landing, territorial seizure or occupation plan. The missing bridge is independently verified ground-force and sustainment activity.",
  "What would change the assessment. The most significant combination would be a verified increase in ground combat units; unusual strategic sealift or equipment prepositioning; expanded regional fuel and ammunition capacity; and theater-specific medical, engineering and command support. Each indicator should be checked against routine rotation and alternative missions before drawing a conclusion.",
  "The human and fiscal stakes. A limited raid, temporary seizure, prolonged occupation and regime-change campaign carry radically different military requirements and costs. American taxpayers would bear operations and replacement costs; Iranian civilians would face risks from fighting and disrupted infrastructure; Gulf neighbors and shipping companies would face wider economic exposure. Estimates should be scenario-specific, not presented as a single forecast.",
  "Our finding as of November 6. The publicly reviewed material does not independently verify the combined logistics and force indicators necessary to conclude that a large-scale U.S. ground invasion of Iran is imminent. That is a statement about available evidence—not proof that no contingency planning exists or that future deployments cannot change the picture. EP News will update the assessment when independently corroborated evidence materially changes it."
],
